  "title": "Psalms: Prayer, Worship, Lament, Wisdom, Justice, Pilgrimage, and Praise",
  "description": "An eight-lesson study of the Psalter as Scripture-shaped prayer for the whole life of faith: delight, danger, grief, guilt, anger, injustice, creation, pilgrimage, kingship, hope, and praise. Psalms gives language to God’s people without requiring every prayer to sound calm or every poem to function as a literal promise.",
  "theme": "The Psalter teaches faithful people to delight, trust, lament, confess, protest, remember, wait, seek justice, worship, and praise before God. A responsible reading respects poetic genre and original context, receives the New Testament’s Christ-centered use of the Psalms, protects vulnerable people, and refuses prosperity formulas, coercive confession, political messianism, or violent imitation of imprecatory language.",
  "audience": "Adults, groups, classes, and ministry teams",
  "purpose": "To read representative psalms from across the collection, learn major prayer and worship genres, distinguish metaphor from guarantee, understand royal and Messianic interpretation carefully, make room for unresolved lament, connect confession with accountability, confront unjust power, approach imprecatory prayers honestly, and let the Psalter form worship that includes both praise and truth-telling.",
  "background": "The Psalter contains 150 psalms arranged in five books (Psalms 1–41; 42–72; 73–89; 90–106; 107–150), with editorial doxologies and a final crescendo of praise. It includes hymns, individual and communal laments, thanksgiving, wisdom, royal psalms, songs of Zion, pilgrimage songs, and prayers for justice. Superscriptions connect many psalms with David, Asaph, the sons of Korah, Solomon, Moses, and particular situations, but questions of authorship and precise setting should be handled with appropriate humility.\n\nPoetry communicates through parallelism, image, hyperbole, metaphor, remembered history, and emotionally intense prayer. Christian readers receive the Psalms within Israel’s Scriptures and also through the New Testament, which repeatedly applies royal and suffering psalms to Jesus. Christ-centered reading should deepen rather than erase the psalm’s earlier literary and historical sense. Trust psalms are not immunity contracts, lament is not weak faith, and prayers for judgment are not authorization for personal or communal violence.",
