      "examination": "Do I rush people toward praise because their lament makes me uncomfortable? Have I treated repeated sadness as proof that someone is not trusting God? Where do I need to stay present longer, ask a careful safety question, or bring my own unresolved prayer honestly before God?",
      "challenge": "Write a short lament using four movements if they fit: address God, name the pain, ask clearly for what you need, and state whatever trust you can honestly say. Do not force a praise ending. If your own prayer reveals that you cannot stay safe, reach out immediately to a trusted person and qualified crisis or emergency support.",
      "caution": "Give advance notice that these psalms contain abandonment, bodily distress, mockery, death, isolation, and darkness. Participants may pass. Do not diagnose from poetic language. If someone expresses current suicidal intent, plan, access to lethal means, inability to remain safe, or imminent danger, follow local crisis procedures and do not leave the person alone. Prayer can accompany professional mental-health and medical care.",
      "prayer": "God who hears cries from the depths, receive our unanswered questions and exhausted hearts. Keep us from forcing quick endings, give us courage to remain present with one another, protect people in danger, and hold us through Jesus, who knows the prayer of abandonment from within. Amen.",
      "openingParagraphs": [
        "Read Psalms 13, 22, 42–43, and 88 and mark how long, forget, enemy, trust, forsaken, cry, mock, bones, garments, praise, deer, thirst, tears, remember, downcast, hope, depths, wrath, friends, grave, and darkness. Notice which psalms change emotional direction and which do not."
      ],
      "contextParagraphs": [
        "Lament is one of the Psalter’s major forms. It typically brings complaint, petition, remembered relationship, and some expression of trust or praise, but no single pattern controls every psalm. Psalm 13 moves rapidly from repeated “how long?” to petition and trust. Psalms 42–43 share a repeated refrain and are often read as a linked composition of longing, memory, self-address, and hope.",
        "Psalm 22 begins with abandonment and moves through mockery, bodily distress, petition, rescue, and worldwide praise. Jesus quotes its opening on the cross, and the Gospel writers connect other details of the crucifixion with the psalm; Christian fulfillment should not erase the psalm’s earlier identity as a real lament. Psalm 88 is especially important because it ends without visible resolution, leaving darkness as the final image."
      ],
      "jesusParagraphs": [
        "Jesus enters lament rather than standing outside it. On the cross He prays the opening of Psalm 22, and Hebrews presents Him as a sympathetic high priest. Christian hope therefore does not require emotional denial; sufferers can bring abandoned-feeling prayer to the One who has Himself prayed from suffering."
      ],
      "guardrailParagraphs": [
        "Do not call lament weak faith, require every testimony to end happily, diagnose depression from a psalm, or tell someone that feeling abandoned means God has abandoned them. Do not treat all intense lament as identical to suicide risk, and do not ignore current self-harm danger because “the Psalms talk that way.” Context and safety both matter."
      ],
      "closingTakeaway": "The lament psalms teach that faith can ask “how long?”, feel abandoned, repeat hope, or end in darkness while still addressing God; the church’s task is not to censor that prayer but to accompany it truthfully and safely."
